   "id": "concept2",
   "type": "concept",
   "title": {
    "en": "Queries, forms and reports"
   },
   "body": {
    "en": "A query asks the database for records that meet conditions, for example all sales above ₹10,000 to one party, written in SQL as SELECT * FROM Sales WHERE Amount > 10000. Forms are screens for entering data correctly, such as a voucher entry form with checks. Reports present the results in a printed or summary form, such as a ledger, a trial balance or a debtors list. A DBMS reduces duplication of data, keeps data consistent, allows many users at once and controls who can see or change what."
   },
   "points": [
    {
     "en": "Query: retrieve records by conditions"
    },
    {
     "en": "Form: controlled data entry"
    },
    {
     "en": "Report: formatted output (ledger, trial balance)"
    },
    {
     "en": "Benefits: less duplication, consistency, security, sharing"
    }
   ],
   "narration": {
    "en": "A query asks the database for records that meet conditions, for example all sales above ₹10,000 to one party, written in SQL as SELECT * FROM Sales WHERE Amount > 10000. Forms are screens for entering data correctly, such as a voucher entry form with checks. Reports present the results in a printed or summary form, such as a ledger, a trial balance or a debtors list. A DBMS reduces duplication of data, keeps data consistent, allows many users at once and controls who can see or change what."
   },
